export const GUIDE_MARKDOWN: Record<string, string> = {
  "zapier": "# Monitor a Zap with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL), and a Zapier plan\nthat includes the **Webhooks by Zapier** app. Webhooks by Zapier is a premium app, so it is not\navailable on Zapier's free plan.\n\nThe idea: add one last step to your Zap that tells StepCanary \"I ran\". If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the Zap, e.g. \"New lead → CRM\".\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the step to your Zap\n\n1. Open the Zap in the Zapier editor.\n2. Click the **+** below the **last** step.\n3. Search for and choose **Webhooks by Zapier**.\n4. For **Action event**, choose **POST**, then **Continue**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Zapier editor, choosing Webhooks by Zapier with event POST ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Payload Type** | `json` |\n| **Data** | Optional. See \"Watch the data too\" below. Leave empty for a simple heartbeat. |\n| **Wrap Request In Array** | `No` |\n| **Unflatten** | `Yes` |\n| **Basic Auth**, **Headers**, **File** | Leave empty |\n\n`[ Screenshot: Webhooks by Zapier POST step with URL and Payload Type filled in ]`\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Test step** in Zapier.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received\"**. No refresh needed.\n3. **Publish** the Zap.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nThat is all. StepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived, so nothing can fire\nwhile you are still setting up.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your Zap… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Runs on a schedule (e.g. every morning at 9) | **Schedule** mode with the same times, and 15-30 minutes of grace. Zapier's schedule trigger can start a few minutes late. |\n| Polls for new items (most Zapier triggers) and normally finds something every hour or so | **Interval** mode. Pick an interval comfortably longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets activity in business hours (lead forms, bookings) | **Interval** mode with an **active window**, e.g. Mon-Fri 09:00-18:00, so nights and weekends never alert. |\n| Runs dozens or hundreds of times a day | **Volume** mode: StepCanary learns what is normal for each weekday and tells you when it drops. |\n\nRemember that the ping only happens when the Zap **runs to the end**. A polling Zap that finds\nnothing new does not run, so does not ping. Choose the interval with that in mind.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nZaps often keep running while a mapped field quietly goes blank. To catch that:\n\n1. In the **Data** section of the webhook step, add a row for each field you care about:\n   type a name on the left (e.g. `email`) and map the real field from an earlier step on the right.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and tick **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape\"**.\n\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each one is filled in, blank, or a different\ntype. It never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Data section of the webhook step with two mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures straight away (optional)\n\nBy default you find out about a failure when the pings stop. If you use Zapier's per-step\n**error handler** (on plans that have it), add a Webhooks by Zapier **POST** step on the error\npath with the URL `…/p/your-token/fail`. StepCanary then alerts immediately instead of waiting.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Test step | The URL must be the whole ping URL with nothing added or missing. Copy it again with the **Copy** button. |\n| Zapier shows a 404 for the step | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| \"Malformed payload\" warning on the check | A mapped value contains a character that broke the JSON. The ping still counted. Setting Payload Type to `json` and using the Data rows (not a hand-typed body) avoids this. |\n| Alerts when the Zap is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell. Lengthen it, add an active window, or switch to Volume mode. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The Zap is running more than 60 times a minute. Check for a Zap that triggers itself. |\n| Filters or Paths in the Zap | Steps after a Filter that stops, or on a Path that was not taken, do not run. Put the ping step where it will run for every case you count as \"working\". |\n",
  "make": "# Monitor a Make scenario with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and a Make scenario.\nThe HTTP app is available on every Make plan. Each ping uses one operation (credit) per run.\n\nThe idea: add one last module to your scenario that tells StepCanary \"I ran\". If those pings\nstop arriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the scenario.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the module\n\n1. Open the scenario in the Make editor.\n2. Click the **+** after the **last** module.\n3. Search for **HTTP** and choose **Make a request**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Make editor, adding the HTTP \"Make a request\" module at the end ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **Headers**, **Query String** | Leave empty |\n| **Body type** | `Raw` |\n| **Content type** | `JSON (application/json)` |\n| **Request content** | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| **Parse response** | `No` |\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP \"Make a request\" module with URL, POST, Raw, JSON and the request content ]`\n\nField names vary slightly between versions of Make's HTTP app. If yours shows \"Body content\ntype\" and \"Body input method\", choose `application/json` and a raw JSON body.\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Run once**.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received\"**.\n3. Save the scenario and make sure its schedule is **on**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your scenario… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Runs at set times | **Schedule** mode with the same times and 10-15 minutes of grace. |\n| Runs every N minutes and always completes | **Interval** mode, a little longer than the scenario's own interval. |\n| Is triggered instantly by a webhook, mostly in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Processes many items a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nIf your scenario runs on a schedule but only reaches the last module when there is data\n(for example the trigger returns no bundles), the ping only happens on runs that had something\nto do. Either pick an interval that allows for quiet runs, or put the HTTP module on a route that\nalways executes.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a mapped field goes blank or missing:\n\n1. Change **Request content** to include the fields you care about, mapping values from earlier\n   modules:\n\n   ```json\n   {\"email\": \"{{1.email}}\", \"order_id\": \"{{1.id}}\", \"total\": {{1.total}}}\n   ```\n\n   Put text values in quotes and leave numbers unquoted.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and tick **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape\"**.\n\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank or a different type.\nIt never stores the values. If a mapped text value contains a quote or line break the JSON can\nbreak; StepCanary still counts the ping and shows a \"Malformed payload\" note. To avoid that, set\n**Body type** to `Application/x-www-form-urlencoded` and add the fields as key/value rows instead.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Request content with mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures straight away (optional)\n\n1. Right-click the module most likely to fail and choose **Add error handler**.\n2. On the error route, add another **HTTP → Make a request** module with the same settings, but\n   the URL `…/p/your-token/fail`.\n3. End the error route with the directive you normally use (e.g. **Break** or **Rollback**).\n\nStepCanary then alerts as soon as the error route runs, instead of waiting for pings to stop.\n\n`[ Screenshot: error handler route with an HTTP module pointing at /fail ]`\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Run once | Did the run reach the HTTP module? Check the bubble above it. Then check the URL is the whole ping URL. |\n| The HTTP module shows a 404 | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| \"Malformed payload\" on the check | See \"Watch the data too\" above. The ping still counted. |\n| Alerts when the scenario is fine | The interval is too short for runs that have nothing to process. Lengthen it or add an active window. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The scenario is pinging more than 60 times a minute, usually because the HTTP module sits inside an iterator. Move it after the aggregator, or to the end of the scenario. |\n",
  "n8n": "# Monitor an n8n workflow with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and an n8n workflow,\non n8n Cloud or self-hosted. A self-hosted instance must be able to make outbound HTTPS requests.\n\nThe idea: add one last node to your workflow that tells StepCanary \"I ran\". If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the workflow.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the node\n\n1. Open the workflow in the n8n editor.\n2. Click the **+** after the **last** node and add an **HTTP Request** node.\n\n`[ Screenshot: n8n canvas with an HTTP Request node added at the end ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n**Parameters tab**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Authentication** | `None` |\n| **Send Query Parameters**, **Send Headers** | Off |\n| **Send Body** | On |\n| **Body Content Type** | `JSON` |\n| **Specify Body** | `Using JSON` |\n| **JSON** | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\n**Settings tab**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **On Error** | `Continue` |\n| **Retry On Fail** | On, 2 tries (optional) |\n\nSetting **On Error** to `Continue` means that if StepCanary were ever unreachable, your\nworkflow would still finish normally. Monitoring should never be the thing that breaks it.\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP Request node parameters ]`\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP Request node Settings tab with On Error = Continue ]`\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Execute step** (called \"Test step\" or \"Execute node\" in some versions).\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received\"**.\n3. Save the workflow and make sure it is **Active**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your workflow… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Starts from a **Schedule Trigger** | **Schedule** mode with the same cron expression and timezone as the trigger, and 5-10 minutes of grace. |\n| Starts from a **Webhook** or app trigger with steady traffic | **Interval** mode, longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets traffic in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Handles many executions a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nIf the workflow has an **IF** or **Switch** node, the ping only happens on branches that reach\nthe HTTP Request node. Connect every branch you count as \"working\" to it.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a field goes blank or missing:\n\n1. Change **Specify Body** to `Using Fields Below` and add one row per field you care about,\n   with an expression as the value, e.g. name `email`, value `{{ $json.email }}`.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and tick **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape\"**.\n\nUsing fields (not hand-written JSON) lets n8n escape quotes and line breaks for you.\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank or a different type.\nIt never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Body parameters with mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures straight away (optional)\n\nn8n can run a separate workflow whenever this one fails.\n\n1. Create a new workflow with an **Error Trigger** node followed by an **HTTP Request** node:\n   Method `POST`, URL `…/p/your-token/fail`.\n2. Save it.\n3. In the workflow you are monitoring, open **Settings** (the ⋯ menu) and choose that workflow\n   as the **Error Workflow**.\n\nStepCanary then alerts as soon as an execution fails, instead of waiting for pings to stop.\nOne error workflow per monitored workflow keeps the tokens separate.\n\n`[ Screenshot: workflow settings with Error Workflow selected ]`\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after executing the node | Check the node's output panel for the response. `{\"ok\":true}` means it reached us; then confirm you are looking at the right check. |\n| The node returns 404 | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| Connection or timeout errors on self-hosted n8n | The server cannot reach the internet over HTTPS. Check its firewall or proxy settings. |\n| Alerts when the workflow is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell, or the Schedule mode timezone differs from the trigger's. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The node runs once per item for a large batch. Open the node's **Settings** and turn on **Execute Once**, or put it after the loop. |\n",
  "pipedream": "# Monitor a Pipedream workflow with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and a Pipedream\nworkflow.\n\nThe idea: add one last step to your workflow that tells StepCanary \"I ran\". If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the workflow.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the step\n\n1. Open the workflow in the Pipedream builder.\n2. Click the **+** below the **last** step.\n3. Choose **HTTP / Webhook**, then **Send any HTTP Request**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Pipedream builder, choosing HTTP / Webhook → Send any HTTP Request ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Auth**, **Params**, **Headers** | Leave empty |\n| **Body** → content type | `application/json` |\n| **Body** → raw content | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\n`[ Screenshot: Send any HTTP Request step with method, URL and body filled in ]`\n\nPrefer code? A Node.js step does the same thing:\n\n```javascript\nexport default defineComponent({\n  async run({ steps, $ }) {\n    await fetch(\"https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/YOUR-TOKEN\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\" },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ status: \"ok\" }),\n    });\n  },\n});\n```\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Test** on the step.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received\"**.\n3. **Deploy** the workflow.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your workflow… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Starts from a **Schedule** trigger | **Schedule** mode with the same cron expression and timezone, and 5-10 minutes of grace. |\n| Starts from an HTTP or app event trigger with steady traffic | **Interval** mode, longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets traffic in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Handles many events a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nIf an earlier step ends the workflow early (for example with `$.flow.exit()` or a filter step),\nlater steps do not run and no ping is sent for that event. Place the ping step where it runs for\nevery case you count as \"working\".\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a field goes blank or missing, send the fields you care about in the body. In\nthe HTTP step, switch the body to key/value fields and reference earlier steps, e.g. key\n`email`, value `{{steps.trigger.event.body.email}}`. In a code step:\n\n```javascript\nbody: JSON.stringify({\n  email: steps.trigger.event.body.email,\n  order_id: steps.trigger.event.body.id,\n}),\n```\n\nThen edit the check in StepCanary and tick **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape\"**.\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank or a different type.\nIt never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP step body with mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures straight away (optional)\n\nIn a Node.js step, wrap the risky part of the workflow and report the failure before rethrowing:\n\n```javascript\nexport default defineComponent({\n  async run({ steps, $ }) {\n    try {\n      // ... your logic ...\n    } catch (err) {\n      await fetch(\"https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/YOUR-TOKEN/fail\", { method: \"POST\" });\n      throw err;\n    }\n  },\n});\n```\n\nStepCanary then alerts immediately instead of waiting for pings to stop.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Test | Look at the step's result. `{\"ok\":true}` means it reached us; then confirm you are looking at the right check. |\n| The step returns 404 | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| Alerts when the workflow is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell, or the Schedule mode timezone differs from the trigger's. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The workflow is running more than 60 times a minute. Check for a workflow that triggers itself, or a source emitting far more events than expected. |\n",
};
